WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.951
When a business buys or sells on credit, the money is paid later. So it keeps an account for every credit customer and every credit supplier, to know who owes what.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:21.956
Every ledger account has two sides. Debit on the left, credit on the right. Each side has a date, the details, and the amount.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:36.987
A customer owes us, so their balance and sales go on the debit side. Money received, discount allowed and returns reduce it, so they go on the credit side. A supplier's account is the mirror image.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:48.746
First, decide: customer or supplier? Then enter the opening balance as balance brought down: debit for a customer, credit for a supplier.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:01:00.122
Next, work out the amounts. Trade discount comes off the list price before anything is recorded, and off returns too. Cash discount is only given for paying in time.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:08.128
Post each transaction in date order. In the details, write the other account or book, such as bank, or purchases day book.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:18.635
Then balance it. Put the difference on the smaller side as balance carried down, write equal totals, and bring the balance down on the opposite side, next day.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:38.589
Here is a real question from November 2025. Sean owes his supplier Sui one thousand five hundred and forty dollars. Sui gives fifteen per cent trade discount on purchases over five hundred dollars, and five per cent cash discount for paying within ten days.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:46.196
We must prepare Sui's account, balance it, and bring the balance down. Eight marks.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:02:00.321
Sean owes Sui, so the opening balance goes on the credit side. On the fourth, six hundred and twenty is over five hundred, so trade discount applies: six hundred and twenty times zero point eight five is five hundred and twenty-seven.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:13.338
On the eighth, Sean pays the opening balance within ten days, so he takes five per cent cash discount, seventy-seven dollars, and pays one thousand four hundred and sixty-three. Both go on the debit side.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:22.233
On the tenth, he returns goods listed at eighty dollars. They had trade discount, so the return is sixty-eight dollars.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:38.622
On the twenty-fifth, he pays the rest: five hundred and twenty-seven minus sixty-eight, four hundred and fifty-nine. That is too late for cash discount. On the twenty-ninth, he buys goods for three hundred and forty. That is not over five hundred, so no trade discount.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:51.279
Now balance. The credit side totals two thousand four hundred and seven. The debit side has two thousand and sixty-seven. The difference, three hundred and forty, is the balance carried down, on the debit side.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:03:03.176
Bring it down on the credit side on the first of August. Sean still owes Sui three hundred and forty dollars. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:12.633
A common mistake is recording the list price. Take the trade discount off first, for returns too.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.992
Another is bringing the balance down on the same side. It always crosses to the other side.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:33.661
Now you try. Maya is a credit customer of Kofi. Read the transactions, then prepare Maya's account in Kofi's books. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:48.405
First the amounts. The sale is four hundred dollars after trade discount, and the return is forty. Maya paid in time, so discount allowed is forty-five, and the bank payment is eight hundred and fifty-five.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:02.394
Maya is a customer, so her balance and the sale are debits, one thousand three hundred in total. Bank, discount and returns are credits. The balance, three hundred and sixty dollars, is brought down on the debit side on 1 June.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:11.484
Remember: customer or supplier, net amounts, post each line, then balance and bring it down. Now try the practice questions.
